+# Privacy implementation and third-party audit
+
+Reviewed: 2026-10-30. Scope: this repository's static HTML, CSS, assets, build tools, and deployment policies. This is a source audit, not a legal assessment or an audit of a running host.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+| Surface | Finding and treatment |
+| --- | --- |
+| HTML and client scripts | No client JavaScript, forms, analytics, pixels, session replay, fingerprints, cookies, localStorage, IndexedDB, or service workers. CSP blocks scripts, workers, forms, frames, and data connections by default. No personal data is collected or retained by application code. |
+| Hero photograph | Previously requested directly from images.unsplash.com, exposing each visitor's network/request metadata. Now downloaded once and served as assets/coffee.jpg. No visitor request reaches Unsplash. |
+| Fonts and favicon | Device fonts and a local SVG; no font CDN or third-party icon service. |
+| Hosting | Hosting necessarily receives IP addresses, request paths, and request headers. Provider, access controls, logging, and retention are deployment decisions; they cannot be verified from this repository. |
+| Existing navigation | Account, rewards, orders, contact, and logout are existing links, not implemented services in this repository. No authentication, payment data, or logout endpoint exists here. |
+| Dependencies | No installed application dependencies and no browser SDKs. Build/audit scripts use only Node built-ins. The existing start command downloads statikk through pnpm dlx; it is a local tooling dependency, not shipped browser code or the production deployment path. It was not executed. |
+
+Photo provenance: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80 (existing site image, retrieved 2026-10-30). Keep this URL in documentation only, not page resources.
+
+## Enforcement and deployment
+
+Run `npm run build` with Node 22.12+ to audit the public source and copy only public files into `dist/`. No server or dependency installation is required. `npm run check:privacy` runs just the source audit, including negative regression fixtures. The scanner is deliberately conservative and is not a comprehensive HTML parser or security sanitizer; manually review every public asset change.
+
+Deploy `dist/` using the included Netlify configuration or the Vercel configuration. `_headers` provides the Netlify/Cloudflare Pages header format; `vercel.json` provides equivalent Vercel rules. `security-headers.json` is the policy reference; the audit rejects drift between those files and both HTML CSP fallbacks. For any other host, explicitly configure the same HTTP headers: ordinary static file servers, including the existing local start command, may ignore `_headers`.
+
+The HTML CSP and referrer meta tags provide fallback protection. Frame embedding controls, permissions restrictions, HSTS, and other response-only protections require the hosting provider to apply the HTTP headers. The `frame-ancestors` directive is intentionally excluded from meta tags because browsers ignore it there. Headers are scoped to this static deployment; review before attaching backend services on the same origin.
+
+Serve over HTTPS with HTTP-to-HTTPS redirects at the host. HSTS starts at 300 seconds to allow a staged rollout; after confirming TLS operation, increase it to 31536000. Do not enable includeSubDomains or preload until every affected subdomain has been reviewed. Confirm actual response headers through deployment tooling before release. No live headers or browser behavior were verified in this task.
+
+No CSP reporting endpoint is enabled: there is no collector here, and collecting full violation URLs or script samples could introduce personal-data logging. Add reporting only with a first-party collector that strips query strings, tokens, identifiers, and samples before storage and has a documented short retention period.
+
+## Secure handling and future review gates
+
+- Keep personal information, tokens, and session identifiers out of URLs, browser storage, analytics, exception messages, and logs. Do not add collection unless a concrete purpose and minimal fields are documented. No encryption keys or secrets belong in public assets.
+- Before launch, configure the hosting provider to disable unnecessary access analytics, omit query strings and credentials from logs, restrict log access, and retain necessary security records only for a documented short period. Update the public privacy notice with the actual provider, retention, and a working privacy contact when these are known.
+- For any future account, order, or contact service, define purpose, minimal data fields, recipient, retention/deletion policy, and access/correction/deletion mechanisms before collecting data. Validate and authorize on the server; use TLS and protected server storage. Explain each requested field near its input.
+- Keep sessions server-managed with Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax cookies, preferably with a __Host- name. A future logout must invalidate the server session via POST with CSRF protection, expire the cookie, and send Clear-Site-Data on its own response. Do not simulate logout by clearing browser storage, or set Clear-Site-Data on every static response.
+- For future DOM updates, use textContent or safe element creation; never interpolate untrusted text into HTML. Changing the no-script policy requires an explicit source and data-flow review, appropriate static CSP hashes or response nonces, and updated automated checks.
+- Review new services, SDKs, dependencies, resource URLs, cookies, storage, permissions, and data recipients on every integration change and before each release. Record purpose, data shared, retention, vendor terms, version, and removal plan. Prefer local assets and static links. Gate optional third-party embeds/analytics behind explicit opt-in with equally easy withdrawal; where appropriate use click-to-load facades and privacy-enhanced endpoints. Consent must precede any third-party request, including thumbnails.
+- If immutable third-party scripts or styles ever become necessary, pin versions, audit transitives, and use SRI plus anonymous CORS. Revisit CSP, Permissions-Policy, popup behavior, public notice, and audit fixtures together rather than broadly allowing external sources.
+
+The automated checks flag departures from today's no-collection architecture. A source audit cannot establish a provider's logging behavior or prove the absence of all possible privacy leaks.
